<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">State of South Haiti</h1><p class="st-meta">1810 CE – 1812 CE · capital Les Cayes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived breakaway state in southern Haiti, set up by the veteran revolutionary André Rigaud and reabsorbed into Pétion's republic in 1812.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-rigaud">André Rigaud</a>, who had led the free people of colour in the south during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> and lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-knives">War of the Knives</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a> in 1800, returned from exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1810. Discontent with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Alexandre Pétion's</a> leadership in the south allowed him to set up a separate government based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/les-cayes">Les Cayes</a> in November 1810.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is recorded of its institutions. It was essentially a regional military government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-rigaud">Rigaud</a>, drawing support from officers and landowners of the southern peninsula, which had long been his stronghold. Like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Pétion</a>'s republic it was dominated by the mixed-race elite and relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and smallholder agriculture.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-rigaud">Rigaud</a> died in September 1811, and command passed to General Jean-Baptiste Borgella. Facing pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-haiti">Kingdom of Haiti</a>, Borgella reconciled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Pétion</a>, and the south rejoined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti</a> in March 1812.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" 